          "distinct": false,
          "id": "68eed335e2261c207e867f42225ce9d659456160",
          "message": "feat(sparq-zk): off-circuit W3C VC cryptosuite ingest bridge + zk:sourceCryptosuite provenance (sq-9c5e) [OPUS-4.8] (#1155)\n\n* feat(sparq-zk): off-circuit W3C VC cryptosuite ingest bridge + zk:sourceCryptosuite provenance (sq-9c5e) [OPUS-4.8]\n\nThe OFF-circuit W3C Verifiable-Credential ingest bridge (design\n`research/zk-configurable-commitment-design.md` §5): verify a source VC's\nW3C Data-Integrity proof (`eddsa-rdfc-2022` / `ecdsa-rdfc-2019`) at the HOST,\nthen re-commit under sparq's commitment pipeline and record the\n`zk:sourceCryptosuite` provenance.\n\n- New OPT-IN `vc-bridge` cargo feature (OFF by default): the default\n  `sparq-zk` build pulls NO ed25519/p256/sha2 dependency and the `vc_bridge`\n  module is compiled out — the lean-default constraint holds (verified by\n  `cargo tree`).\n- `vc_bridge`: `VcCryptosuite::{EddsaRdfc2022, EcdsaRdfc2019}`,\n  `verify_source_proof` / `verify_source_proof_by_token` (real Ed25519 /\n  ECDSA-P256 verify over `proofConfigHash || documentHash`, fail-closed),\n  `ingest_verified_vc` (verify FIRST, then re-commit) -> `IngestedCredential`.\n- `zk:sourceCryptosuite` registry slot (IRI const + `RegistryEntry`\n  `source_cryptosuite` field + `with_source_cryptosuite` + (de)serialize +\n  round-trip test). The slot is always present; the feature gates the bridge\n  that POPULATES it.\n\nHonest scope (documented loudly): provenance / back-compat ONLY — NOT a\nre-verifiable in-proof property (the query proof binds to\n`Poseidon2SchnorrV1`, not the VC's own proof, §5.3). NOT in scope: in-circuit\nVC verification, `bbs-2023` / `ecdsa-sd-2023` selective disclosure (deferred\nseam), P-384 ECDSA, JSON-LD processing, `did:` resolution. NOT externally\naudited (sq-qhy4); asserts no in-circuit / query-soundness property.\n\nJudgment calls (steer post-hoc): (1) the bridge operates over the credential's\nRDF graph (its canonical N-Quads — the form the DI suites hash) rather than\nbundling a JSON-LD processor; (2) `zk:sourceCryptosuite` records the verbatim\nW3C `proof.cryptosuite` token as a plain literal (a bare suite token, not an\nabsolute IRI). Both documented for the maintainer in an open issue.\n\nGates (both feature states): cargo build / clippy --all-targets -D warnings /\ntest GREEN with the feature OFF (default) AND ON (`--features vc-bridge`);\nrustdoc --workspace --no-deps --all-features (RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings) clean;\ncheck-privacy-claims clean; check-readme-template --enforce 0 deviations\n(README stays a 30-line internal stub); typos clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(supply-chain): vet 10 new RustCrypto p256-tree crates for vc-bridge [OPUS-4.8]\n\ncargo vet regenerate imports pulls hmac:0.12.1 from the ISRG upstream\naudit set. The remaining 9 RustCrypto transitive deps (crypto-bigint,\necdsa, elliptic-curve, ff, group, p256, primeorder, rfc6979, sec1) have\nno matching upstream audit; added [[exemptions]] safe-to-deploy entries\nin supply-chain/config.toml with notes identifying these as\nRustCrypto-org published crates gated behind the OFF-by-default\nvc-bridge feature. `cargo vet --locked` now passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(supply-chain): promote serdect 0.2.0 exemption to safe-to-deploy\n\nThe vc-bridge feature promotes the p256 dependency tree from\nsafe-to-run to deployable; serdect 0.2.0 (transitive via the p256\npath) was the one crate left at safe-to-run, failing cargo-vet.\nMirror the sibling RustCrypto exemption entries (ecdsa,\nelliptic-curve, p256, primeorder) with the standard justification\nnote. `cargo vet --locked --frozen` passes locally.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Jesse Wright <jmwright.045@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <jesse@jeswr.org>\nCo-authored-by: GPT-5.6 <noreply@openai.com>",
